Last Saturday, Daniel and his classmates took part in a community project to improve a local park. The students originally planned to begin at nine o'clock, but heavy rain caused them to delay the activity until ten.
When they arrived, their teacher divided them into three groups. One group collected rubbish, another planted flowers, and the third group repaired several wooden benches. Daniel wanted to plant flowers, but he joined the group repairing the benches because they needed someone who knew how to use basic tools.
The work was more difficult than the students expected. Some of the benches were badly damaged, and one group discovered that they had brought the wrong type of paint. Instead of giving up, they contacted the school and arranged for the correct materials to be delivered.
By three o'clock, the park looked completely different. The students were tired, but they were proud of what they had achieved. Daniel said that the most important lesson was not about gardening or repairing furniture. He realized that successful projects often depend on people cooperating when unexpected problems appear.
